  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1116 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe strong upper low to our west continues to slowly shift east\nand will moves across the state tonight and tomorrow. The\nairmass contains a lot of moisture, even a bit more than\nyesterday. That means heavy rainfall is possible with stronger\nshowers and storms, or in some cases multiple storms over the\nsame area. There is still a risk of strong to severe storms,\nalthough things may a bit \"less volatile\" than Thursday, BUT the\nrisk of hail and gusty winds is still there...and SPC continues\nwith a MARGINAL RISK for severe storms through tonight. For\nSaturday, showers and storms will continue...but we will also\nsee some pockets of dry air across the area which is typical\nwith this type of low. Amounts across the Magic Valley, South\nHills, and Albion Mountains are the \"lowest\", with up to 0.25\"\nin some places. Even where amounts are lower, there will some\npocket of moderate to heavy rainfall...which could lead to\nlocalized flooding issues. Elsewhere, amounts in excess of 0.50\"\nare expected over a larger area. Of course, higher amounts are \npossible. We issued a more widespread FLASH FLOOD WATCH through\nthis evening, based on 24 hour rainfall amounts and where we saw\nflooding taking place. The only places not included are the\nMagic Valley, Albion Mountains, and South Hills.  \n\nSome wraparound showers and storms are expected Sunday, mainly\naround Island Park and Driggs. Otherwise, it will be mostly dry\nthrough next week. The exception will be potentially Tuesday\nand/or Wednesday. The next low swings across the Great Basin\nduring that period. The models are still wavering on how far\nnorth with the track, which could bring a few showers and storms\nespecially around Bear Lake. This is actually shown in the Blend\nof Models forecast.",
